#:blog{:title "Beyond Monolithic Runtimes: Languages as Distributed Tuples and Streams"
       :date #inst "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000-00:00"
       :abstract [:p "How datom.world eliminates monolithic C/FFI runtimes by executing guest languages over three pure host modules and content-addressed prelude streams. This post covers the host seam, DHT-published preludes, composable compilation perspectives, and virtual POSIX without ambient OS syscalls."]
       :content [:section.blog-article
                 [:div.section-inner
                  [:article
                   :$blog-title
                   :$blog-date

                   [:p "When modern operating systems run a programming language, whether Python, TypeScript, Java, or Ruby, they drag along baggage: monolithic C runtimes, vendor-specific FFI bindings, complex " [:code "sys.path"] " resolution, ambient mutable environments, and platform-specific POSIX system calls. Moving a running program to another machine or a browser tab quickly hits a wall."]

                   [:p "With exact integers to a declared bound, strict IEEE-754 carrier discipline, and mutation-tested parity across JVM, Node.js, and Dart VM, the groundwork is laid for the linked prelude runtime. Here is how " [:code "datom.world"] " rethinks the fundamental nature of programming language runtimes."]

                   [:h2 "1. The Seam: Three Modules for Every Language"]
                   [:p "A common misconception is that running Python inside the Yin VM requires writing a slow Clojure emulator for Python. In reality, the architecture cleanly separates execution from host acceleration:"]
                   [:ul.bulleted
                    [:li [:strong "Python runs as native guest code: "] "Python syntax, variable scoping, inheritance, generators, and exception handling are compiled into Universal AST and executed natively by the Yin VM."]
                    [:li [:strong "The Host Seam is three pure modules: "] "The host seam is three pure modules (" [:code "cell"] ", " [:code "data"] ", and " [:code "integer"] ") with 55 exports between them. The entire Python prelude reaches the host through 40 of those names plus the VM's 22 core primitives. Nothing in the prelude calls a host function outside these:"]
                    [:ul.bulleted
                     [:li [:code "cell/*"] " (3 exports): " [:code "cell/new"] ", " [:code "cell/get"] ", and " [:code "cell/set!"] ", powering all mutable heap objects (lists, dicts, instances, and the runtime context)."]
                     [:li [:code "data/*"] " (26 exports): string primitives over UTF-16 units and Unicode code points, collection slicing, memory bounds (" [:code "data/max-items"] "), and float64 carrier discipline."]
                     [:li [:code "integer/*"] " (26 exports): exact arithmetic bounded by declared bit and digit limits, bitwise logic, radix parsing, and shortest-digit float formatting."]]]

                   [:p "The architectural bet is that a second language reuses these same three modules. The prelude manifest declares the host modules it needs by content address, so when the JavaScript or Scheme prelude lands, anyone can verify whether the bet held directly from its published manifest."]

                   [:h2 "2. What a Prelude Is"]
                   [:p "The Yin VM understands only lambda calculus, continuations, effects, and the cell and stream modules. It knows nothing about Python classes, JavaScript prototype chains, or Java vtables."]
                   [:p "A " [:strong "prelude"] " is the language-specific foundation automatically loaded before user code. It defines the personality and semantics of the language:"]
                   [:ul.bulleted
                    [:li [:code "py/add"] ": Decides whether " [:code "+"] " performs exact integer addition, list concatenation, or raises a " [:code "TypeError"] "."]
                    [:li [:code "py/truthy"] ": Codifies that " [:code "[]"] ", " [:code "{}"] ", and " [:code "0"] " are falsy in Python (whereas in JavaScript, " [:code "Boolean([])"] " is true)."]
                    [:li [:code "py/make-class"] " and " [:code "py/getattr"] ": Implements single-inheritance attribute lookup and dynamic dispatch."]]

                   [:p "Consider evaluating a simple expression like " [:code "a + b"] ". In conventional runtimes, this relies on C-level dispatch tables. In Yin, it lowers into clean guest applications of prelude functions:"]
                   [:pre
                    [:code
                     ";; Lowered form of `a + b` in Universal AST\n"
                     "[-1 :yin/type :application]\n"
                     "[-1 :yin/operator -2]\n"
                     "[-1 :yin/operands [-3 -4]]\n"
                     "[-2 :yin/type :variable]\n"
                     "[-2 :yin/name py/add]\n"
                     "[-3 :yin/type :variable :yin/name a]\n"
                     "[-4 :yin/type :variable :yin/name b]"]]
                   [:p "Notice that no host names or C bindings appear in this lowered form. The operation is expressed entirely in terms of the language prelude."]

                   [:h2 "3. Preludes on the DHT: Languages as Distributed Data"]
                   [:p "In conventional systems, installing Python requires a binary install of hundreds of megabytes on disk. In " [:code "datom.world"] ", " [:strong "code is content-addressed data (Axiom 3)"] "."]
                   [:p "The architecture treats the Python prelude as a linker module named " [:code "py"] ": compiled once into semantic rows, published content-addressed to the " [:code "dao.jing.dht"] " peer-to-peer store, and required by every Python program:"]
                   [:ul.bulleted
                    [:li "A lightweight edge node or browser tab with zero Python installed can execute Python code on demand."]
                    [:li "When a task requires " [:code "'py"] ", the Linker fetches the content-addressed prelude tuples over the DHT, verifies their cryptographic hash, and links them."]
                    [:li "A new program pins a new hash, while a running task keeps the image it started with. A published package pins its prelude, so it runs the same way forever without breaking legacy code."]]

                   [:p "The resulting module structure links explicitly and deterministically:"]
                   [:pre
                    [:code
                     ";; Module shape with linked prelude\n"
                     "(do (require 'py)              ; hoisted content delivery\n"
                     "    (yin/def spec {:name \"app\" :package? false})\n"
                     "    (yin/def body (fn [%globals %globals-fn]\n"
                     "                   <lowered module body>)))"]]

                   [:h2 "4. Composable Stream Compilation: One Stream, Many Perspectives"]
                   [:p "Code is stored as canonical semantic tuple rows: the stored payload in " [:code "dao.jing"] " is the flat body " [:code "[tag & slots]"] ", and the full logical row " [:code "[id tag & slots]"] " is reconstructed on read. Because code is pure data, multiple independent observers can read the exact same stream concurrently, each completely ignorant of the others:"]
                   [:ul.bulleted
                    [:li [:strong "AST Walker: "] "Reconstructs map trees for interactive inspection and structural reflection. (The macro expander is simply one more observer on the syntax side of a stream boundary.)"]
                    [:li [:strong "Semantic VM: "] "Linearizes rows into a positional instruction vector for fast evaluation via a CESK state loop."]
                    [:li [:strong "Register VM (de Bruijn): "] "Eliminates variable names into register offsets for execution speed."]
                    [:li [:strong "Datalog Relation ($ast): "] "The same rows are indexed by " [:code "dao.space.index"] ", and " [:code "yin.repl"] " exposes them as the " [:code "$ast"] " relation to relational Datalog queries (" [:code "dao.space.query/q"] ")."]]
                   [:p "This symmetric ignorance is the master design's load-bearing invariant: the evaluator never consults an index to run, and the index never evaluates a form to index it. (For deep dives on how rows and linearization work, see " [:a {:href "/blog/ast-datom-streams-bytecode-performance.blog"} "AST Datom Streams"] " and " [:a {:href "/blog/universal-ast-vs-assembly.blog"} "Universal AST vs Assembly"] ".)"]

                   [:h2 "5. Host Boundaries and Virtual POSIX: No Ambient Syscalls"]
                   [:p "What about operating system operations like opening files, network sockets, or reading timers?"]
                   [:p "Per the Host Boundaries specification (rooted in " [:strong "Axiom 1: Everything is a Stream"] "), " [:code "datom.world"] " strictly rejects ambient OS syscalls: all host contact is a stream boundary, effects out, events in. Here is where the architecture points for POSIX capabilities:"]
                   [:ul.bulleted
                    [:li "File descriptors (" [:code "fd"] ") are task-local indices pointing to " [:code "dao.stream"] " handles."]
                    [:li "File contents are content-addressed blobs in the same " [:code "dao.jing"] " store that holds code; directories are stream-backed trees over them."]
                    [:li "A network socket is a dial or a serve over " [:code "dao.stream.remote-channel"] ", which is itself a stream."]
                    [:li "Processes and signals are Yin VM continuations and safepoint event streams."]]
                   [:p "The continuation format (UCF) already gives a paused task a content-addressed identity and wire form (see " [:a {:href "/blog/computation-moves-data-stays.blog"} "Computation Moves, Data Stays"] "). Once heap lift lands in future development, a paused Python task will carry its objects with it and resume elsewhere across the network without leaving any dangling OS file descriptors."]

                   [:h2 "Conclusion"]
                   [:p "By treating languages not as monolithic C runtimes, but as " [:strong "content-addressed semantic tuples over universal streams"] ", " [:code "datom.world"] " establishes a disciplined foundation: cross-platform determinism today, and verifiable multi-language mobility across the distributed web tomorrow."]]]]}
